# Preset policy for this image.
#
# A preset file is the one place an "off" decision survives the first boot.
# The image ships without /etc/machine-id on purpose - jh7110-prepare creates
# one per board, so no two boards share an identity - and systemd reads a boot
# with no machine id as a first boot: it runs a preset pass over /etc before
# anything else starts, and that pass rewrites the enabled state of every unit
# from the preset policy.  It happens on the board, after the build is long
# finished, so a unit the build disabled by hand is enabled again the first
# time the board boots.
#
# The policy is why the default answer is the wrong one.  A preset file names
# the units the distribution wants switched on and says nothing about the rest,
# and a unit that no rule matches is enabled.  An image that wants a unit off
# therefore has to say so here; leaving it unnamed is the same as asking for
# it.
#
# The name sorts before 90-systemd.preset, which is what it has to be: the
# first rule that matches a unit is the one that counts.

# smartd is useful when a SMART-capable disk is attached, but it is not a boot
# prerequisite and it exits noisily on the SD/eMMC-only boards this image is
# written to.  smartmontools stays installed; its daemon is opt-in.
#
# Nothing here is the package's doing: smartmontools' own postinst enables the
# daemon, which is a deliberate default for a server and the wrong one for a
# board whose only disk is the card it booted from.
disable smartmontools.service

# NetworkManager is the network stack this image ships, configures and writes
# /etc/resolv.conf for, and it is the one the first-run setup drives over
# D-Bus.  systemd-networkd is a second stack for the same job, and nothing
# asks for it but the preset default above: no package enables it, and no
# .network file matches this board's ethernet - the ethernet example in
# /usr/lib/systemd/network is an .example, which does not load.  Left running
# it manages nothing, and systemd-networkd-wait-online.service then waits for
# connectivity that never arrives, times out after 120 seconds and fails, on
# every boot, holding up everything ordered after network-online.target - while
# NetworkManager-wait-online.service has the same target satisfied in seconds.
#
# systemd-networkd-persistent-storage.service is not named here because a
# preset rule cannot reach it: it has no [Install] section, so it is static and
# is pulled in by whatever pulls in networkd.  It is bound with
# BindsTo=systemd-networkd.service, so it goes only where networkd goes.
disable systemd-networkd.service
disable systemd-networkd.socket
disable systemd-networkd-wait-online.service
